# the changes game mutants lets by, one per line as NAME :: WHY. NAME
# is the file, the function and the change, as a report prints it after
# the file and line: no line, since lines move.
hole/march.go ClosestApproach: if point, momentum, givenUp = hole.pacedStep( point, momentum, radius, bounds, ); givenUp { break } => if point, momentum, givenUp = hole.pacedStep( point, momentum, radius, bounds, ); givenUp { } :: a given-up step hands the ray back where it stands, so each step after it is given up the same way and the least radius does not move: only how many steps are taken before they run out differs
hole/numbers.go package: cubicDegree = 3 => cubicDegree = 4 :: the degree is the cubic's slope in noReturn's Newton's method; a slope of 4 y^2 in place of 3 y^2 takes shorter steps from above, never past the root, so it closes on the same root more slowly and stops within a few last bits of it, below what noReturn's answer is good to
hdf5/chunks.go checkChunks: index = index*grid[axis] + at/across => index = index*grid[axis] - at/across :: each step's index is then the negative of the true one, modulo 2^64, by induction from the first axis; negation is one to one, so the same chunks meet in seen and the count of chunks is the same
hdf5/datatype.go package: signedBit = 0x08 // defined: bit 3 of the fixed-point field => signedBit = 9 // defined: bit 3 of the fixed-point field :: 9 adds bit 0, the big-endian bit, and fixedPoint refuses a big-endian integer before it reads the signed bit, so bit 0 is always clear there
hdf5/file.go package: noAddress = 0 => noAddress = 1 :: findLink gives noAddress only beside an error, and its one caller returns on the error without reading the address
hdf5/filter.go package: noID = 0 // defined: no filter has id 0 => noID = 1 // defined: no filter has id 0 :: readFilter gives noID only beside an error, and its one caller returns on the error without reading the id
hdf5/globalheap.go parseObjects: walk.short || size > room => walk.short || size >= room :: size equal to room is the whole body, and 16 bytes of the object's header are read before it is taken, so the take is short and walk.short is already true
hdf5/group.go package: noLevel = -1 // defined here: the level a tree's root takes => noLevel = -2 // defined here: the level a tree's root takes :: a level is read from one unsigned byte and a child wants its parent's less one, so no level wanted or read is negative, and any negative noLevel compares the same
fits/header.go package: bits = 64 => bits = 65 :: strconv.ParseFloat reads at 32 bits only when told 32, and at 64 for any other size, so 65 reads every card as 64 does
fits/image.go imageOf: header.Has("BLANK") && bitpix > noPixels => header.Has("BLANK") && bitpix >= noPixels :: a BITPIX of none is refused by widthOf before this line is reached, so no image here has one
fits/image.go Open: section := io.NewSectionReader(file, at, math.MaxInt64-at) => section := io.NewSectionReader(file, at, math.MaxInt64+at) :: io.NewSectionReader takes a length that runs past the end of int64 as no limit, so both read to the file's end
fits/sky.go standard: toward <= equator => toward < equator :: the two differ only for a place exactly a quarter turn from the tangent point, where the cosine in float64 is 6e-17 and never none, so no place lands on the edge
fits/sky.go apart: return onceRound(to-from+halfTurn) - halfTurn => return onceRound(to-from-halfTurn) - halfTurn :: adding half a turn and taking it away differ by one whole turn, which onceRound takes away
fits/image.go package: shortOfOne = 1 // defined: what rounds a size up to whole blocks => shortOfOne = 2 // defined: what rounds a size up to whole blocks :: the two differ only for data one byte past whole blocks, where the walk lands on the last block, holding that byte and the zeros the standard pads with. read as a header it has no cards, so the walk reads on to the next header and finds the same image
grissom/mesh/geometry.go package: firstTip = 0 => firstTip = 1 :: every corner of an icosahedron has the same five nearest at the same angle, so the edge measured from the second corner is the edge measured from the first
grissom/mesh/geometry.go isEdge: return math.Abs(apart-edge) <= edgeSlack => return math.Abs(apart-edge) < edgeSlack :: an edge differs from the nearest by about 1e-16 radians and a corner that is no edge by more than half a radian, so nothing lands on the slack itself
grissom/mesh/geometry.go outward: shape.first.dot(shape.second.cross(shape.third)) < none => shape.first.dot(shape.second.cross(shape.third)) <= none :: a face of the icosahedron spans a fifth of a hemisphere, so its triple product is never none
grissom/mesh/geometry.go within: return way.dot(normal(shape.first, shape.second)) >= none && way.dot(normal(shape.second, shape.third)) >= none && way.dot(normal(shape.third, shape.first)) >= none => return way.dot(normal(shape.first, shape.second)) > none && way.dot(normal(shape.second, shape.third)) >= none && way.dot(normal(shape.third, shape.first)) >= none :: a place exactly on that edge leaves this cell for the one across it, where the edge comes second or third and its test is still not strict, so the plain search finds it there and the key moves to that neighbour: hippo's probe of 2026-11-02 moved 3,992 keys of 259,880 places to depth 27. both hold the place, and Locate promises one of the cells that share an edge, so either is it; TestPlacesAlongEdgesAreFoundAtEveryDepth holds the place inside each edge of the cell it is put in
grissom/mesh/geometry.go within: return way.dot(normal(shape.first, shape.second)) >= none && way.dot(normal(shape.second, shape.third)) >= none && way.dot(normal(shape.third, shape.first)) >= none => return way.dot(normal(shape.first, shape.second)) >= none && way.dot(normal(shape.second, shape.third)) > none && way.dot(normal(shape.third, shape.first)) >= none :: a place exactly on that edge leaves this cell for the one across it, where the edge comes first or third and its test is still not strict, so the plain search finds it there and the key moves to that neighbour: hippo's probe of 2026-11-02 moved 1,996 keys of 259,880 places to depth 27. both hold the place, and Locate promises one of the cells that share an edge, so either is it; TestPlacesAlongEdgesAreFoundAtEveryDepth holds the place inside each edge of the cell it is put in
grissom/mesh/geometry.go within: return way.dot(normal(shape.first, shape.second)) >= none && way.dot(normal(shape.second, shape.third)) >= none && way.dot(normal(shape.third, shape.first)) >= none => return way.dot(normal(shape.first, shape.second)) >= none && way.dot(normal(shape.second, shape.third)) >= none && way.dot(normal(shape.third, shape.first)) > none :: a place exactly on that edge would leave this cell for the one across it, as the strict first and second edges do; hippo's probe of 2026-11-02 moved no key of 259,880 places to depth 27. both hold the place, and Locate promises one of the cells that share an edge, so either is it; TestPlacesAlongEdgesAreFoundAtEveryDepth holds the place inside each edge of the cell it is put in
grissom/mesh/geometry.go nearest: if found := slices.IndexFunc(shapes, way.within); found >= none { return found } => if found := slices.IndexFunc(shapes, way.within); found >= none { } :: with no early return the greatest margin is taken every time. within holds when margin is none or more but for rounding, so for a place within rounding of an edge the greatest margin can be the cell across it, and the key moves there: hippo's probe of 2026-11-02 moved 2,394 keys of 259,880 places to depth 27. both cells hold the place to rounding; elsewhere the two choices are one. both hold the place, and Locate promises one of the cells that share an edge, so either is it; TestPlacesAlongEdgesAreFoundAtEveryDepth holds the place inside each edge of the cell it is put in
grissom/mesh/geometry.go nearest: found >= none => found > none :: when within finds the first triangle, the margins choose instead, and for a place within rounding of its edge they can choose the cell across it, so the key moves there: hippo's probe of 2026-11-02 moved 26 keys of 259,880 places to depth 27. both cells hold the place to rounding. both hold the place, and Locate promises one of the cells that share an edge, so either is it; TestPlacesAlongEdgesAreFoundAtEveryDepth holds the place inside each edge of the cell it is put in
grissom/mesh/geometry.go nearest: way.margin(shapes[i]) > way.margin(shapes[best]) => way.margin(shapes[i]) >= way.margin(shapes[best]) :: differs only when no triangle holds the place and two margins are equal to the last bit; then each is as near as the other, and either is the nearest Locate promises; hippo's probe of 2026-11-02 moved no key
